  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nHurricane Isaias continues to approach the northern Gulf Coast\nthis afternoon, with landfall expected over the western Florida\nPanhandle this evening. Across our area, the westernmost extent of\nouter rain bands are gradually increasing into southeast and east\nMississippi, and conditions are becoming more breezy. Heading into\ntonight, gusts will continue to increase across the Pine Belt.\nConfidence in sustained tropical storm force wind is low, but\nthere will be an increasing chance for wind gusts to tropical \nstorm force after sunset. As Isaias moves northward overnight and\nbegins to transition to an extratropical system, an area of \nstronger wind is expected to develop to the west of the center. \nIndividual CAM solutions have indicated gusts upward into the \n60-70 mph range in pockets, with specific areas impacted shifting \nsomewhat from run to run. While some of these individual solutions\nmay be a bit overbaked, CAM ensembles do lend credence to the \nlikelihood of stronger gusts beginning overnight across east MS \nand expanding to areas as far west as the MS River during the \nmorning Saturday. 12Z REFS probabilities for 50+ mph wind gusts \nare as high as 70-80 percent in the Golden Triangle area by mid- \nmorning Saturday, with 30+ percent probabilities in the Meridian \narea as early as 3am, shifting up to the MS 25 corridor through \nmidday. The existing Tropical Storm Watch was upgraded to a \nTropical Storm Warning, with Choctaw and Webster counties added to\na warning as well. There is some potential for a second maximum \ncorridor across the eastern Delta or I-55 corridor, though there \nis less confidence in the most extreme version of this scenario \npresented by the 12z HRRR. Regardless of the peak wind gusts, \nwinds will be consistently gusty across most of the area during\nthe daytime Saturday. A Wind Advisory has been posted for most \nareas not under a Tropical Storm Warning, except for our AR \ncounties and our westernmost LA parishes. Downed trees and power \nlines are likely, especially in the east and northeast MS where\nthe highest gusts are anticipated over the longest duration, so \nscattered power outages are a near certainty.\n\nRain, on the other hand, will not be as plentiful across our area.\nREFS PMM from this morning still shows a swath of 1-3\" across the\nGolden Triangle. A Flood Watch remains in effect for this area,\ngiven the worst case potential for up to 4-5\" in a short period \nof time. But as you go farther south and west, totals drop off \nsubstantially. It is a near certainty that some of our southeast \nAR, northeast LA, and southwest MS will receive no rain. Rain will\ntaper off across the entire area by evening. Clouds are expected\nto provide enough temp relief to avoid major fire danger issues \nin the dry areas tomorrow, but by Sunday and Monday, RHs below 30\npercent will become a reality again, especially for our areas \nalong and west of the MS River. Fortunately winds will be lighter,\nbut we'll still have to monitor fire danger potential heading \ninto next week.\n\nNotably, in the areas that do receive rain through much of the \nday Saturday, temperatures will struggle to reach 70.\n\nDry weather is expected through much of next week, but showers\nwill be possible again around Friday ahead of a cold front. After\nsome heat relief tomorrow, for most of next week temperatures \nwill run well above normal to near record at some sites. However,\nwith dewpoints primarily in the 60s, these conditions will feel \nmostly tolerable, and mornings will still be slightly cool to \ncomfortable. /DL/",
